From df1dffd54da6097e2377d914b796f7f99b74de09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 10:56:57 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] evd_colocation.jl: where the enhanced diffusion fires under
 ice, against the net surface flux; melt_lens: net freshening
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

analysis/evd_colocation.jl (new; CPU, run's own output, no GLORYS): per under-ice column and day, the fraction of the
day the faces at the bottom of the top two cells are unstable (daily-mean κᶜ / κᵉᵛᵈ: κᶜ is exactly κᵉᵛᵈ = 100 m²/s
there), classed by the column's net surface freshening that day (brine / neutral / melt) and by the daily-mean N² of
the top pair (near-neutral or stratified); the corrected surface fluxes by region (net, ice part, rest; heat); the top
cell's T − Tₘ; and the initial state against the sea ice's liquidus (supercooled cells, ice made by the first frazil
step). evd_colocation.md, evd_colocation.png (maps, days 1–10).

Fix in melt_lens.jl: NumericalEarth's net ocean Jˢ holds only the ice's own salt, Jˢ = Eᵢ Sᵢ / ρ₀ (Eᵢ > 0 when the
ice grows); the dilution S Jʷ is applied in the salinity boundary condition. The table showed Jˢ / S as "freshening":
the opposite sign to the ice's net effect and ~4 times too small. Now Jʷ + Jˢ / S (η from the flux file; the ice's
part Jˢ (1/S − 1/Sᵢ) if η is missing). Also κᶜ and e (on faces) are placed at face depths, not cell centres.

Why (26 Nov 2026): the 30-day flux run's melt_lens.md read as brine under ice in early September; corrected, those
columns are melting (2–8 mm/day of fresh water) and the Central Arctic is freezing from day 1.

